@section ffstreamer

Read an input whose every byte a parent process serves, by offset, over two
pipes it hands down. A sandbox that leaves FFmpeg no path to the file and no
socket then still lets it read its input: the parent decides every read, and
storage that stops answering holds the parent, which can always end the run,
rather than FFmpeg waiting in the kernel.

The parent speaks the ffstreamer protocol, specified at
@url{https://github.com/kstenerud/ffmpeg-ffstreamer}. It creates the two pipes,
hands FFmpeg one end of each, and names them with the options below. The URL is
exactly @code{ffstreamer:}, with nothing after it. Reading only; each pipe
serves one session at a time.

An open refused for its options leaves the two descriptors as they were: one
missing, the same descriptor twice, stdin, stdout or stderr, one that is no
pipe or open the wrong way (@code{EINVAL} or @code{EBADF}), or a pipe that
serves another session already (@code{EBUSY}). Otherwise the descriptors are
the session's, and the protocol closes them when the session closes or the
open fails.

It needs @code{sigtimedwait()}, so it is not built on macOS or OpenBSD.

This protocol accepts the following options:

@table @option
@item ffstreamer_read_fd
The descriptor the parent's messages arrive on. Required.

@item ffstreamer_write_fd
The descriptor this side's messages go out on. Required.

@item ffstreamer_cache_blocks
How many of the parent's blocks to keep, from 2 to 1024. Default 32.
@end table

The parent must restrict the input to this protocol, since a nested open
could otherwise name one of the channel's descriptors with @code{pipe:} or
@code{fd:}:
@example
ffmpeg -protocol_whitelist ffstreamer -ffstreamer_read_fd 4 -ffstreamer_write_fd 5 -i ffstreamer: ...
@end example

